use /lib
from /scope/prompt-mode/modes import PromptModes

export anchor MessageListBackgrounds:
    description:
        The header for the latest task shows where it came from by its mode's
        colour, of the @{PromptModes}: a bar in that colour at its left
        and a faint tint of it behind it, as its sent prompt says, never a
        strong fill. A task from the history that doesn't say which mode it
        was sent in has no bar and keeps the tab bar's colour.

        Each task in the previous tasks list hints at the mode it was sent in:
        its heading's background is a faint tint of that mode's colour, of the
        @{PromptModes}, red for Code, purple for Chain, blue for Spec,
        green for Ask, and grey for Freeform, far fainter than the latest
        task's header, so the list reads as a column of the list's own
        background with each task only touched by its colour. A task whose
        mode isn't known, as one from the history that doesn't say, keeps the
        list's background untinted. Its status label, prompt, checkbox, and
        buttons stay readable over the tint, in light and dark mode, and its
        hover and open states still show over it. An open task's prompt and
        output beneath its heading are not tinted.
